---
import AuthorCard from "../components/AuthorCard.astro";
import { AUTHOR, SITE } from "../consts";
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";

const jsonLd = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "AboutPage",
  name: `About ${SITE.name}`,
  url: new URL("/about/", Astro.site ?? SITE.url).href,
  description: SITE.description,
};
---

<BaseLayout
  title={`About — ${SITE.name}`}
  description={`Who writes ${SITE.name}, who it is for, how the analysis is produced, and what we refuse to do.`}
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
>
  <div class="container">
    <main id="main">
      <div class="page-head">
        <h1>About {SITE.name}</h1>
        <p>
          We read China's trade policy in the original, then translate it into
          the only language that matters to a buyer: landed cost per unit.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="prose" style="margin-top:32px;">
        <h2>What this site does</h2>
        <p>
          China publishes an enormous amount about its own trade — tariff
          schedules, customs bulletins, platform rule changes, industrial
          statistics. Almost all of it is written for a domestic audience, in
          language built for policy readers rather than buyers.
        </p>
        <p>
          {SITE.name} is the translation layer. We take the primary documents,
          find the numbers that matter, and work out what they cost on a
          per-unit and per-container basis — for the person placing the order,
          not the person writing the press release.
        </p>

        <h2>Who this is for</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>Platform sellers.</strong> Amazon, Temu, TikTok Shop and D2C
            operators in the US and EU who need to know what a rule change does
            to their margin this quarter. Start with{" "}
            <a href="/category/platforms/">Platforms</a> and{" "}
            <a href="/category/trade-policy/">Trade Policy</a>.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Importers and buyers in emerging markets.</strong> Businesses
            across Southeast Asia, the Middle East, Africa and Latin America
            sourcing from China — where the practical questions rarely get
            answered in English, and almost never with numbers attached. Start
            with <a href="/category/sourcing/">Sourcing &amp; Suppliers</a> and{" "}
            <a href="/category/logistics/">Logistics &amp; Customs</a>.
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          The two audiences want different things from the same policy. We write
          for both, and we say which one a piece is for.
        </p>

        {/*
          The id is the anchor the article bylines point at.

          Every Article block carries author.url = /about/#who, and Google's guidance
          treats a byline as more credible when it resolves to a page explaining who the
          author is. Without this id the link resolved to the top of this page instead,
          which made the claim weaker than the markup implied.
        */}
        <h2 id="who">Who is behind this</h2>
        <p>
          {AUTHOR.name} — {AUTHOR.role}. Years on the sourcing and platform side
          of cross-border commerce: Amazon FBA, TikTok Shop and Temu, in consumer
          electronics and home goods. Close enough to the factory floor to know
          when a number in a bulletin will not survive contact with a real quote.
        </p>

        <h2>How the analysis is produced</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>Numbers first.</strong> Every article carries at least five
            precise figures with a value, unit, date and named official source.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Landed-cost math.</strong> Policy news is only useful once it
            is converted into FOB price × duty rate × container volume.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>A clear position.</strong> "Most sellers assume X. The math
            says Y. Do Z." No both-sides hedging.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Named sources only.</strong> Ministry of Commerce, General
            Administration of Customs, National Bureau of Statistics, Xinhua,
            CCTV — plus the destination market's own customs authority. No links
            farmed from other blogs.
          </li>
        </ul>

        <h2>What this site is not</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>Not a mouthpiece.</strong> We are not affiliated with, funded
            by, or speaking for any government, chamber or trade body. We explain
            Chinese policy because understanding it is the whole job — not
            because we are promoting it.
          </li>
          <li>Not a news aggregator — no rewriting somebody else's story.</li>
          <li>Not financial or legal advice.</li>
          <li>
            Not ad-funded at the expense of the reader: no tracking pixels, no
            sponsored posts pretending to be analysis.
          </li>
        </ul>

        <h2>Corrections</h2>
        <p>
          If a figure is wrong, it gets corrected and the change is recorded in a
          dated note on the article itself, under Corrections and updates. The
          note states what changed, not just that something did, and the
          machine-readable date the article was last modified moves only when
          that note is added — never on a schedule. Email{" "}
          <a href={`mailto:${AUTHOR.email}`}>{AUTHOR.email}</a>.
        </p>
        <p>
          Every article also carries an internal review date: the point at which
          its central figure is expected to have moved. Some of these are hard
          deadlines published by the authority concerned — a duty rate that
          changes on a named day, a quota period that ends. When one arrives, the
          article is rechecked and either updated with a note or left alone
          deliberately. Nothing is re-dated to look fresh.
        </p>
      </div>
    </main>

    <aside class="sidebar">
      <AuthorCard />
    </aside>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
